  "source": "The AI Providers' own published prices for their APIs, in US dollars. Claude: Anthropic's first-party API prices (platform.claude.com pricing); cache writes are the five-minute rate, 1.25 times input. DeepSeek: api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing, at its peak-hour prices (off-peak is half); cache writes are not charged apart, so are priced as input. Z.ai: docs.z.ai/guides/overview/pricing; cache storage is free for now, so cache writes are priced as input. Minimax: platform.minimax.io/docs/guides/pricing-paygo, pay-as-you-go, at the standard tier and MiniMax-M3's price for prompts up to 512K tokens; its OpenAI-format API, which the Gateway uses, charges no cache writes apart, so they are priced as input. Kimi: platform.kimi.ai/docs/pricing/chat; kimi-k3's cache writes at the five-minute rate, the others' priced as input. OpenRouter publishes its prices through its Models list, so they are not here, except Jev's (typesafe/jev-1.13, the Decision Model), which that list leaves out: $0.042 per million input tokens and nothing for output, as OpenRouter's and TypeSafe's Jev pages give it; it has no prompt cache, so cache writes are priced as input and cache reads, which never happen, at nothing.",
